<h3>TC-DWN-07: Access Revocation &amp; Signed URL Expiry</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Role:</strong> Administrator &amp; Customer A</li>
<li><strong>Prerequisites:</strong> Customer A owns the book; Administrator window open.</li>
<li><strong>Starting Page:</strong> Administrator window at <code>/admin/e-book-customers</code>.</li>
<li><strong>Numbered Actions:</strong><ol>
<li>In Administrator Window, open <code>/admin/e-book-customers</code>.</li>
<li>Search for Customer A (<code>STAGING_TEST_STUDENT_EMAIL</code>).</li>
<li>Open Customer Details $\rightarrow$ Under Entitlements, click <strong>&quot;Revoke Access&quot;</strong>.</li>
<li>Enter reason: <code>Compliance manual test revocation</code> and submit.</li>
<li>In Customer A&#39;s window, refresh <code>/my-books</code>.</li>
<li>Attempt to download the PDF or read chapters.</li>
</ol>
</li>
<li><strong>Expected Results:</strong><ul>
<li>Card displays a red badge: <strong>&quot;Access Revoked&quot;</strong>.</li>
<li>Reader and download buttons are replaced with notice: <code>&quot;Access for this book has been revoked or refunded.&quot;</code></li>
<li>Subsequent requests to <code>/api/ebooks/[bookId]/download</code> return HTTP 403 Forbidden.</li>
<li><strong>Important Technical Context:</strong> Previously downloaded PDF files stored on the user&#39;s hard drive cannot be remotely deleted. A signed URL already issued to the browser may remain functional until its 60-second signature expires. Revocation guarantees <strong>no new signed URLs</strong> can ever be issued.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Evidence to Record:</strong> Screenshot of <code>/my-books</code> displaying &quot;Access Revoked&quot;.</li>
<li><strong>Actual Result:</strong> Pending execution.</li>
<li><strong>Status:</strong> NOT RUN</li>
<li><strong>Issue Reference:</strong> None.</li>
</ul>
